usage-argv: 32-bit PowerPC has no 64-bit atomics, so std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64 does not exist there ("Unable to
resolve `use` target std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64" at src/install.rs:727; hk 2.12.0 with usage-argv 7.3.0; rustc on
ppc32 fails the same way). It is the crate's only 64-bit atomic: `static NEXT` in temp_beside(), a per-process
sequence number that makes the scratch file `<name>.usage-<pid>-<n>.tmp` unique, used only through
`fetch_add(1, Relaxed)`. AtomicUsize is enough: usize is 64 bits on 64-bit targets (same numbers and names there), and
on 32-bit targets the count wraps only after 2^32 scratch files in one process, while each scratch file is renamed
away (or removed) by the replace() call that made it. The same two lines are in every release 6.0.0..7.4.0 at three
different line numbers, so there are three files, each exact (no offset, no fuzz) on its own range:
usage-argv@6.0.0..6.1.0, usage-argv@6.1.1 and usage-argv@6.2.0+. 5.1.0 and earlier have no src/install.rs and no
64-bit atomic.
This file: 6.2.0 and later (exact on 6.2.0..7.4.0, the newest release). Base: usage-argv 7.3.0.

diff -ruN a/src/install.rs b/src/install.rs
--- a/src/install.rs
+++ b/src/install.rs
@@ -724,8 +724,8 @@
 
 /// A scratch path in the target's own directory, so the rename cannot cross a filesystem.
 fn temp_beside(path: &Path) -> PathBuf {
-    use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};
-    static NEXT: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64::new(0);
+    use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};
+    static NEXT: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);
 
     let name = path
         .file_name()
